invariant: INV-17
file: p256_scalar.c
catches: proof/prove-one.sh p256_scalar
reason: p256_scalar_add returns without wiping its sum. The signer's one
  call adds z to r*d, and that sum gives the private key to whoever holds
  the signature. The array's lifetime ends with the call, so no vector
  reads it, and whether a word of it stays on the stack depends on the
  compiler: under gcc 13.3 at -O1 for x86-64 one did, and at -O2 none.
  proof/p256_scalar_harness.c counts the bytes ct_wipe is handed and
  requires two arrays' worth after the call. Each run is a proof, so this
  belongs to the nightly's test-invariants-proof-backed job.

--- old
    reduce_once(o->word, sum, carry);
    ct_wipe(sum, sizeof sum);
--- new
    reduce_once(o->word, sum, carry);
